[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A settled ruling stays visible at every entry point until the corpus is migrated, and drift is corrected on sight

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[INVARIANT]
UID: INV-SETTLED-STAYS-VISIBLE
TITLE: A settled ruling stays visible at every entry point until the corpus is migrated, and drift is corrected on sight
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
A ruling that is accepted but not yet applied to the corpus stays VISIBLE from
every entry point a session reads first -- the skill, the README, and the node
that owns the migration -- and contributors read and cite nodes THROUGH the
ruling, never through the legacy spelling the corpus still carries.

Settled vocabulary is used consistently once it is settled. Drift is corrected
on sight, in one clause, and that includes drift in the operator's own words:
the correction is how the term gets learned.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
A corpus lags its rulings by design -- migration is a deliberate, costed move
and not a drift. The hazard is that between ruling and migration the old
spelling is everywhere and the new meaning is in one node, so a session reading
the corpus at face value re-derives the pre-ruling world and argues from it.
Visibility at the entry points is the cheapest defence, and correcting drift
aloud is what stops a term from decaying back into the old one.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Recorded 2026-08-28 at the operator's request, as one of four contributor-communication requirements (INV-DECISIONS-ARRIVE-AS-CARDS, INV-LISTS-WHERE-THE-READER-UNPACKS, INV-FILE-DO-NOT-NARRATE, INV-SETTLED-STAYS-VISIBLE). The rule is the operator's; AUTHORED_BY reads llm because the writer has no flag for the other value. An INVARIANT is read as a REQUIREMENT under DEC-INVARIANT-IS-A-REQUIREMENT; the grammar type is kept so the grid is not half-applied. The sdoc skill's backlog protocol is the procedure these four are the requirement for.
<<<
